## Common Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Mixing HTTP Logic
```typescript
// DON'T - HTTP status codes in service
async getItem(id: string): Promise<{ status: number; data: any }> {
  if (!id) {
    return { status: 400, data: { error: "ID required" } };
  }
  // ...
}
```

### ❌ Weak Typing
```typescript
// DON'T - Using 'any' types
async processData(data: any): Promise<any> {
  return data.map((item: any) => ({ ...item }));
}

// DO - Use proper interfaces
interface InputData {
  id: number;
  name: string;
}

async processData(data: InputData[]): Promise<ProcessedData[]> {
  return data.map(item => ({
    id: item.id,
    name: item.name.trim(),
  }));
}
```

### ❌ Missing Error Context
```typescript
// DON'T - Generic error handling
async getItem(id: string): Promise<Item> {
  try {
    return await itemModel.findById(id);
  } catch (error) {
    throw error; // No context added
  }
}

// DO - Add meaningful context
async getItem(id: string): Promise<Item> {
  if (!id) {
    throw new Error("Item ID is required");
  }
  
  try {
    const item = await itemModel.findById(id);
    if (!item) {
      throw new Error(`Item with ID ${id} not found`);
    }
    return item;
  } catch (error) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to retrieve item: ${(error as Error).message}`);
  }
}
```

## Benefits of TypeScript Service Pattern

1. **Compile-time Safety**: Catch type errors before runtime
2. **Better IDE Support**: Autocomplete and refactoring tools work better
3. **Self-Documenting**: Interfaces serve as documentation
4. **Easier Refactoring**: TypeScript helps identify all places that need updates
5. **Contract Enforcement**: Interfaces ensure consistent data structures
6. **Enhanced Testing**: Type-safe test data and assertions

